ladspa: drain the real buffer when reducing latency, cap it instead of panicking

On an underrun run() pushes one frame (hop size) of zeros into every output
queue and adds a frame to proc_delay. The "reduce delay again" branch is meant
to undo that, but `o_q.iter_mut().take(self.frame_size)` iterates over the
channels, so it popped a single sample per channel while still subtracting a
whole frame from proc_delay. The real latency never came back down; only the
counter did, so the `proc_delay >= sr` guard never fired either. Every underrun
added 10 ms of latency for the rest of the plugin's lifetime (seconds after a
day or two of occasional underruns).

- Drop a whole frame from every channel when reducing the latency.
- Reduce only while proc_delay is above the initial one-frame buffer (plus
  'Min Processing Buffer'); with `>=` the fixed drain would empty the buffer and
  run() would then wait for the worker in every cycle.
- Cap the added latency at 1 s instead of panicking: the guard is reachable now,
  and a panic in run() aborts the host (e.g. a PipeWire filter-chain process).
  The error is logged once when the cap is reached; underruns at the cap are
  only counted (no log line per cycle from the audio thread) and the count is
  logged when the latency decreases again.
- Count the time since the last latency change in samples instead of run()
  calls, which matched seconds only at a 480-sample host block size (PipeWire
  runs 1024 by default). Keep the 10 s hold after an underrun, but take further
  steps every 1 s instead of every 10 s, so a burst of underruns no longer takes
  many minutes to drain.
- Log latency changes at warn level (visible with the default log filter), so
  the log is a time series of the plugin's latency.

The new unit tests drive run() with the worker thread stopped, using half-frame
host blocks, and check that the queued samples follow proc_delay through
underruns and recovery, with hold and step times in samples, and that the cap
holds without a panic. The thread-count test ignores the test harness threads,
and all tests run one at a time (shared model, thread counting).

